      "dom_id": "question-67335",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the $21$st term of the following arithmetic sequence.\n\n$$\n-3,\\,-7,\\,-11,\\,-15,\\,…\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nFirst, we will write the formula for the $n$th term of this sequence. From the first few terms\n\n$$\n-3,\\,-7,\\,-11,\\,-15,\\,…,\n$$\n\nwe see that the first term is ${a}_{1}=-3$ and the common difference is \n\n$$\nd=-7-(-3)=-4.\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-3+(n-1)(-4) \\\\  & =-3-4n+4 \\\\  & =1-4n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use our formula to find the $21$st term. All we have to do is substitute $n=21:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =1-4n \\\\ {a}_{21} & =1-4(21) \\\\  & =1-84 \\\\  & =-83\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $21$st term of the sequence is ${a}_{21}=-83.$",
